A||||17||sqoach||Figured taking the door pockets off would be easy, but...||||1||
Z||000000||sqoach||11-26-2001||02:50 PM||||I don't know how to take them off! I have the door panel off, look at the back, and the nuts or whatever they are that hold it on won't come off. I don't know what to use. Help!<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>               <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigsqoach2.gif">              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">               <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||kevin||11-26-2001||02:59 PM||||It's not as difficult as it may appear. Once the door panel is off, there are 5 or 6 3/8" or 5mm (approx.) nuts holding the door pockets to the door. WHat I did was s-l-o-w-l-y unscrew them one at a time with the above mentioned wrench. The "bolt" attachment from the door pocket is rathy flimsy and not meant to be tourqued at all. You say you could not find the nuts? Keep looking they are there. Let me know if you have additional questions. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ali_Gulf_Fiero||11-26-2001||03:06 PM||||I hate auto interiors!<BR>Here's what you do: wrench the nuts off with vicegrips then cut the &lt;bolt&gt; down flush. Dremel drill new holes in a keyhole slot pattern then use cap head screws. voila!<BR>That's what I did.<P>------------------<BR>my cat's sitting in my scrambled eggs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||11-26-2001||03:06 PM||||Are those stamped nuts screwed on a plastic stud?<P>Those can be a pain sometimes when they don't want to back out.  Try to get a door handle tool, maybe a 1" paint scraper or a screwdriver under the nut as you turn it.  Bout all I can think of.||||reg||1
Z||000004||sqoach||11-26-2001||06:08 PM||||It looks as though someone put the nuts on backwards. The studs are metal. The nuts look like they are a 5mm (as mentioned in a previous post) but inverted. I don't see how they were put on like that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||11-26-2001||06:16 PM||||when there stripped out some people hammer them on backwards with a deep socket and hammer. Just cut the plastice studs off and you can put them back on with some upholstery screws from the front in the ends, inside the flaps, inside the pocket. Youll never know their there.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phil||11-26-2001||09:27 PM||||Just pulled two pockets off tonight. Really helps if you use a pliers to yank out the padding under the nuts. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||WhiteFieroBlur||11-27-2001||01:10 AM||||I went thru taking the carpet off and pockets man that is one sticky mess cause I sprayed it with WD-40, it comes off so easy but it leaves a goo mess. I just took a screw driver (flat head)and worked it under the screw around all its sides to push it away and bend it from the board and took a wrench and it just screws out like normal, you just have to get up under that screw first cause its so flat. its better if you pull the foam from around each screw first.||||reg||1
Z||000008||cadaver||11-28-2001||12:47 AM||||I think some of the people that have replied have never taken off the DOOR poskets and I am not too sure what they are talking about.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||11-28-2001||01:04 AM||||I'll admit I haven't removed door pockets before, but I should know this because I've changed door pads about 30 times and I know I've looked at those fasteners. LoL.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Just trying to remember exactly what they look like in my head, uhh.  <P>Sqoach, I've been meaning to pull the door pad on mine for the 31st time because in a hurry I forgot to hookup the door lock linkage when I had it off last.  I'll take a look.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000010||Tigger||11-28-2001||01:40 AM||||That was quick.  Well sort of, spent most of the time farting around with the stupid linkage getting the locks to work smoother.<P>I'd try prying up carefully under the  nut with a screwdriver on two sides.  Then use the vise grip method.  I wonder if there's a tool to remove those upside down stamped nuts???<P>The curiosity was eating at me and I got one thing off my to-do list.     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 11-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||clayatandalusia||11-28-2001||01:46 AM||||the hood emblim is the same fastiner<BR>so is the map pocket in the passinger side i jsut pried them up with a thin slotted screwdriver but i remembered them a 10 socket..  i know i had to use a deep and i turned it wiht my hand||||reg||1
Z||000012||tgowens||11-28-2001||03:15 AM||||if the nuts are inverted, use a pair of needle nose pliers to remove them -- I do it that way at junk yards all the time......||||reg||1
Z||000013||sqoach||11-28-2001||05:09 PM||||YES! Got them off! I used a pair of needlenose pliers to loosen them, then some regular pliers to get a better grip and just twist them off.<P>Thanks everyone for your advice!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000014||cadaver||12-03-2001||09:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by clayatandalusia:<BR><B>the hood emblim is the same fastiner<BR>so is the map pocket in the passinger side i jsut pried them up with a thin slotted screwdriver but i remembered them a 10 socket..  i know i had to use a deep and i turned it wiht my hand</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ah, No they are not.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||fieroparts.com||12-04-2001||12:15 AM||||They are a pain in the ....||||reg||14
Z||000016||cadaver||12-04-2001||11:25 AM||||why does every one say there a pain. They come off just fine with vise grips.||||reg||1
Z||000017||286SE'S||12-05-2001||03:14 PM||||If you use vice grips, screwdrivers and pliers the wooly insulation will get messed up and the nut will get bent. My brother in law made me a tool for these as I we redoing my interior and was replacing the door panels. He took a piece of solid steel pin and drilled a hole in one end. Then ground down that end into a six sided shape that fits down into the inverted fasteners. I held it in vice grips to remove them but he could have gone a little further and made it fit my cordless driver but as was for a one time use no need to. <BR> It works great.<BR>There must be special tool for these. There is no need for them to be inverted in the first place. A 10mm deep drive socket can be used to install them if you don't need to be factory original.||||reg||1
